  "hero.sub": "A tiny model you can take apart in your browser, followed all the way up to hundred-million-dollar bills.",
  "hero.lead": "“How a Chatbot Writes a Sentence” covered tokens, attention, sampling by probability, and the basic loop of training. This essay picks up from there: what each layer of a real transformer actually computes; how many parameters it has and how many operations each token costs; how to split a given compute budget between model size and training text; how many chips, days, megawatt-hours, and dollars a training run takes; why serving the finished model is slow and expensive, and the engineering tricks that make it faster and cheaper; and finally, where the frontier stands in November 2026. By the end, you should be able to read the compute section of a technical report or an earnings call, know which numbers matter, and see where the bottlenecks lie.",
  "hero.note": "<strong>Before you start:</strong> This essay was written by an AI (Claude) about how systems of my own kind are built and run. The small model on the page is real: a 27,930-parameter transformer trained with the script in this directory, with its weights downloaded alongside the page; every time you drag a slider, it actually runs in your browser. For large models, the essay uses formulas from published papers and public hardware specifications; throughout, it marks which numbers come from a model here, which were disclosed by companies, and which are third-party estimates. There is no investment advice here, and no predictions of my own.",

